/* Exam 3 "Hear the why": spoken explanations for q_NEW3.js, q_GAP3.js and the core items of q_WHY_L13.js, q_WHY_L14.js, q_WHY_L15.js. Loaded after narration3.js. */
Object.assign(NARR_Q, {
"NEW3-001": "Tolerance is not one of the five principles of toxicology. The five principles are source, exposure, A D M E T, dose, and target and mechanism of action. Source is where the toxin or toxicant comes from. Exposure is how people or critters are exposed. A D M E T is absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion and toxicity. Dose is the dose makes the poison. Target and mechanism is where the substance acts and how it harms. Tolerance belongs to the general concepts of undesired effects. It is decreased responsiveness after a prior exposure, with two mechanisms, dispositional tolerance and reduced responsiveness. In class she said, toxicologists think of these five principles. We think about the source of a toxin or toxicant. We think about how are people or critters exposed.",
"NEW3-002": "The incorrect statement is that every compound has a dose below which it causes no harm. Some dose-response curves have a threshold, a dose below which no response is seen, and the S-shaped curve has one. But carcinogens were long modeled with a straight line from zero and no threshold, on the idea that a single molecule damaging D N A might start a cancer. In class she said, don't always assume everything has an area where a dose is not harmful. Not everything is like this. The other statements are correct. The saying that the dose makes the poison is credited to Paracelsus, and the L D fifty is the dose that kills fifty percent of the animals, read from a population dose-response curve.",
"NEW3-003": "The incorrect statement is that most injuries to the central nervous system are reversible. Whether a toxic injury is reversible depends on the tissue's ability to regenerate. The liver has a high ability to regenerate, so most liver injuries are reversible. Differentiated nerve cells cannot divide and be replaced, so most central nervous system injuries are irreversible. In class she said, most of our nerves or C N S cells, their injury is permanent. They can't divide. They can't be regenerated. Cancer and birth defects are also considered irreversible. Treatment kills cancer cells but does not turn them back into normal cells, and a malformed body part cannot form again.",
"NEW3-004": "An enantiomer hitting an unintended receptor is off-target, so it is the exception. An on-target adverse effect happens at the drug's intended receptor. Either the dose at that receptor is too high, from a dosing error, a change in the drug's pharmacokinetics, or a change in the number of receptors, or the correct receptor is hit in the wrong tissue, as with sleepiness from diphenhydramine. Off-target effects come from receptors the drug was not meant to reach. Racemic thalidomide is the enantiomer example: the S enantiomer is a potent teratogen. A change in pharmacokinetics is the tempting wrong pick, but it is on-target, because it raises the dose at the intended receptor.",
"NEW3-005": "Hepatotoxicity is organ specific, so it is the one that is not non-organ specific. The non-organ-specific responses are fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis. The organ-specific ones are named for their organ: pulmonary toxicity, neurotoxicity, hepatotoxicity and nephrotoxicity. Fibrosis can form in the lung or the liver, yet it is non-organ specific because the scar tissue has the same chemistry wherever it forms. In class she said, if you look at the chemistry of that fibrotic tissue, it's the same. So the same pathology goes on to create that fibrosis. Cancer is non-organ specific because one mechanism can cause cancers in different organs, and teratogenesis because the defect depends on when in gestation the exposure happens.",
"NEW3-006": "Being a large organ is not a reason the liver is susceptible to toxicity. The reasons are these. The liver takes up and concentrates compounds in its cells. It does a lot of metabolism, which may bioactivate compounds to toxic metabolites. It has its own inflammatory cells, the Kupffer cells, which release inflammatory compounds and reactive oxygen species while cleaning up. It tends to have idiosyncratic reactions. And it receives a large volume of blood through the portal circulation, so an oral dose goes straight to it. About size she said, it's a large organ, yeah, that really doesn't have anything to do with it, right?",
"NEW3-007": "Pulmonary edema is an acute response, so it is not chronic. The lung has two acute responses and three chronic ones. The acute responses are airway reactivity, which is bronchoconstriction that narrows the airways, and pulmonary edema, the exudative phase that thickens the barrier between the alveoli and the capillaries. The chronic responses are fibrosis, emphysema and asthma. Fibrosis adds collagen and makes the lung less plastic. Emphysema makes the lungs larger and too plastic, with hyperinflation. In class she said that airway reactivity and pulmonary edema are acute responses to inhaling things that are bad for our lungs, and that asthma is considered a chronic response. Asthma is the tempting pick, but she classed it as chronic.",
"NEW3-008": "The incorrect statement is that the blood-brain barrier is continuous throughout the brain. It is not continuous; in some parts of the brain it is looser than in others. The other statements are correct. Compounds must pass into the membranes of the endothelial cells to cross it, transporters move some compounds back out to the blood, and how well a compound gets in depends on how lipophilic it is. The barrier is also still developing at birth. In class she said, there are parts of the brain where the blood-brain barrier is looser than others, and that it is still developing at birth for several months to years. So a medication that stays out of an adult's brain may reach a newborn's brain.",
"NEW3-009": "Steatosis is not a mechanism of acute renal failure. It is fatty liver, one of the kinds of liver response. The mechanisms of chemically induced acute renal failure are prerenal causes, vasoconstriction, crystalluria, tubular toxicity, endothelial injury, glomerulopathy and interstitial nephritis. Crystalluria is listed for sulfonamides, acyclovir and ethylene glycol. Interstitial nephritis is listed for antibiotics, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and diuretics, and she added the proton pump inhibitors. In class she said, these are specific kinds of renal damage that can happen. The kinds of damage are the point, more than the compounds under each one. The trap here is a liver term placed among kidney terms.",
"NEW3-010": "The incorrect statement is that a drug with a large volume of distribution is easy to remove by hemodialysis. A volume of distribution greater than five liters per kilogram means the drug is not staying in the blood. It is sequestered in places like lipid, muscle and bone, and dialysis only reaches the blood. In class she said, it's not staying in the vasculature, right? The other statements are correct ways kinetics change in overdose. Exceeding protein-binding capacity raises the free fraction of drug. Saturating liver metabolism lowers the first-pass effect, so more drug reaches the circulation. And high concentrations can change elimination from first order to zero order, which extends the half-life.",
"NEW3-011": "Inactivation of the poison is not one of the four toxicokinetic methods. It is its own approach. The three basic approaches to treating a poisoning are toxicokinetic based, inactivation of the poison, and pharmacologically based. The four toxicokinetic methods are prevention of absorption, inhibition of toxication, also called bioactivation, enhancement of metabolism, which is detoxification, and enhancement of elimination. All four change what the body does with the poison, so less of it, or less of its toxic form, stays in the body. Inactivation instead binds a poison already in the circulation, as chelators and antibodies do, and the complex is cleared. Inhibition of bioactivation is the tempting wrong pick, but fomepizole for methanol is exactly that toxicokinetic method.",
"NEW3-012": "A single binding site for one metal atom is not a characteristic of a chelator. Chelators are usually multidentate: one molecule binds a single metal atom at several sites. They are small molecules with a nucleophilic electron donor that forms a complex with lead, mercury, cadmium, iron or copper. A chelator must bind the metal with higher affinity than the body's own macromolecules do, must be water soluble, non-toxic and readily cleared, and ideally has low affinity for endogenous ions such as calcium. In class she said, it has a multidentate structure, usually is what our chelators have, and, the chelating agent must have a higher affinity for binding the toxic metal than macromolecules naturally in your body.",
"NEW3-013": "Supplying a cofactor for metabolism is not a pharmacological category. It is enhancement of metabolism, a toxicokinetic method. The four pharmacologically based treatments are these. The treatment is a receptor antagonist, such as naloxone, flumazenil or atropine. The treatment is a receptor agonist, such as physostigmine. The treatment removes the poison from the active site, such as pralidoxime. And the treatment by-passes an inhibited pathway, such as vitamin K for warfarin. Giving a cofactor speeds up an enzyme's reaction, as sodium thiosulfate does for the body's enzyme rhodanese in cyanide poisoning. That changes the kinetics of the poison, not its action at a receptor.",
"NEW3-014": "The incorrect statement is that ion trapping is routinely done for weakly basic toxins. It is not done for them. Ion trapping prevents tubular reabsorption. Sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, a weakly acidic toxin becomes charged in that urine, and the charged form is not reabsorbed, so it leaves the body. It is very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital. Trapping a weak base would require acidic urine, and that means acidifying the blood. In class she said, we don't tend to do this for poisons that are weakly basic, because acidifying a person's blood to try to get their urine more acidic is kind of dangerous.",
"NEW3-015": "Tachycardia is not part of beta-blocker overdose. Beta-blocker overdose slows the heart. Its findings are hypotension, bradycardia, altered mental status and respiratory depression. Tachycardia belongs to tricyclic antidepressant overdose, and to iron, where low blood pressure and a fast heart rate appear six to twenty four hours after the ingestion. The altered mental status is what separates beta-blocker overdose from calcium channel blocker overdose. Both give low blood pressure and a slow heart rate, but the calcium channel blocker patient is often alert. Respiratory depression is the tempting wrong pick because it sounds like an opioid finding, but it is listed for beta blockers.",
"NEW3-016": "Elevated serum potassium is not part of tricyclic antidepressant overdose. It belongs to digoxin overdose, with bradycardia, arrhythmias, and nausea and vomiting. Tricyclic antidepressant overdose gives tachycardia, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures, sometimes vasodilation, an elongated Q R S interval and reduced cardiac contractility. Treatment is intravenous fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine, mostly supportive care to keep up blood pressure, and sodium bicarbonate for the elongated Q R S interval, because it counteracts the inhibition of cardiac sodium channels. The fast heart and wide pupils set it apart from the digoxin picture of a slow heart and high potassium.",
"NEW3-017": "The incorrect statement is that serum digoxin levels guide further doses of the antibody fragments. After digoxin-specific antibody fragments are given, the measured total digoxin, free plus bound to the fragments, goes up, so that measurement cannot be used to calculate more doses. The fragments bind digoxin, and the complex is eliminated by the kidneys. The other statements are correct. N-acetylcysteine loses effectiveness about eight hours after an acetaminophen overdose but is still given past that point. Fomepizole is a stronger inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase than ethanol and has fewer adverse effects. And naloxone can trigger withdrawal in a chronic opioid user.",
"NEW3-018": "Each person carrying two alleles of each gene is true, but it does not complicate the link between genotype and phenotype. It is simply what a genotype is: the two alleles a person has for a gene. The complications are these. Many polymorphisms may be present in a single gene, so it is hard to tell which variation causes the phenotype. Most traits are multigenic, not monogenic, so one gene rarely explains a trait. And traits can be influenced by environmental factors, which are gene-environment interactions. A phenotype is any observable characteristic. In class she said, most traits are multigenic, not monogenic.",
"NEW3-019": "The incorrect statement is to induce vomiting at home before transport. For both beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose found out of hospital, the guidance is: do not induce emesis; consider activated charcoal if there are no contraindications; and do not delay transport to the hospital to give charcoal. A patient who is symptomatic when found goes to a hospital, and a patient who needs the hospital is brought by ambulance. In class she said, you don't make them barf at home, it's too dangerous. Charcoal is the tempting wrong pick, but it is allowed, as long as it does not hold up the trip to the hospital.",
"NEW3-020": "Inhalation is not a listed human route of exposure to pharmaceuticals in water. For humans the routes are drinking the water, eating fish, and skin contact during recreation, such as swimming, and drinking water is the main one. For aquatic wildlife the routes are drinking the water, eating food from the water, and the skin. Wildlife are exposed for their whole lives, repeatedly, and constantly if their home range stays contaminated, which is why aquatic wildlife exposure is the more significant. Inhalation sounds plausible because it is a major route for other toxicants, but no inhalation route is listed for drugs in water.",
"NEW3-021": "True. Detoxification is metabolism of a compound to something less harmful that the body can eliminate more easily. Metabolism can go two ways. The other way is bioactivation, metabolism to something more harmful than the parent compound, also called toxication or metabolic activation. Acetaminophen shows both. Most of a normal dose is conjugated by the glucuronosyl transferases and sulfotransferases, which is detoxification. The part that goes through cytochrome P four fifty two E one becomes N A P Q I, which injures the liver, and that is bioactivation. In class she said, we'll use the word detoxification. Again, that's metabolism to something that's less nasty, something better able to be eliminated from the body.",
"NEW3-022": "False. Metabolism does not always make a compound less toxic. It can make something more harmful than the compound a person was exposed to, and that is bioactivation, also called toxication or metabolic activation. This is why toxicologists watch the metabolism step of A D M E closely. Her classic example is acetaminophen: cytochrome P four fifty two E one turns it into N A P Q I, which is toxic to the liver. In class she said, the metabolism part where a compound might be metabolized to something even nastier. I use the word bioactivation for that. Metabolism that does make a compound less harmful and easier to eliminate is detoxification, so the word always is what makes the statement false.",
"NEW3-023": "False. A compound that never reaches a toxic blood concentration can still cause an adverse response. In her repeated-dose example, compound C is cleared between doses and never enters the toxic zone. Still, it could harm a person through an allergic reaction, which needs very little drug, through toxicity at a different target site, or through synergy with other drugs. In class she accepted all three, saying of synergy, could synergize with other drugs. That's great. A related point is that a compound cleared before the next exposure may still have chronic adverse effects. Staying below the toxic concentration rules out accumulation, not every kind of harm.",
"NEW3-024": "False. A poison can cause both a local and a systemic effect, though few do. A local effect happens at the site of first contact, as with chlorine gas or an acid. A systemic effect needs absorption and distribution, and most toxins act systemically unless they are highly reactive. Tetraethyl lead is her example of both. It got through a glove and burned the skin of the hand, the local effect, then was absorbed through the burned skin and damaged the central nervous system, the systemic effect. In class she said, weirdly, some compounds do have both local and systemic effects, very few. I like to give the example of tetraethyl lead.",
"NEW3-025": "True. Cancer and birth defects are considered irreversible toxic effects. Reversibility depends on whether the injured tissue can regenerate and return to normal. A cancer can be cut out or killed with radiation or chemotherapy, but the cells that became cancerous never turn back into normal cells; they are removed. A body part that formed wrongly in a fetus cannot be made to form again. In class she said that cancer and birth defects are an undesired effect with certain exposures, and those are considered irreversible as well. The trap is thinking that a treatable cancer is a reversible one. Treating it removes the cells; it does not reverse the change.",
"NEW3-026": "True. Mutagenicity means a genetic alteration that is transmissible, that can be passed down to offspring. Genotoxicity is the broader term: damage to D N A in general, in somatic cells or in germ cells, and it is not itself transmissible. When the mutations occur in germ cells, they can be passed down, and the agent is called a mutagen. In class she said, and mutagenicity refers to the fact that it's transmissible. In other words, you can pass that down to your offspring. The results of genotoxicity include cancer, teratogenesis and genetically based disorders. The trap is treating the two words as synonyms; damage in a somatic cell is not passed down.",
"NEW3-027": "False. A given teratogen does not always cause the same birth defect. The time of exposure during gestation influences the type of effect. In the hamster study, a retinoid given to the mothers on different days of gestation produced small eyes, exencephaly, spina bifida, cleft palate, or limb and tail defects, depending on the day. The reverse also holds: different compounds can cause the same defect, because the fetus is sensitive to each at a different time. Cleft palate peaks with methanol on gestation day seven and with the dioxin T C D D on day eleven. That timing is why teratogenesis is classed as non-organ specific. In class she said, depending on the time of the mother's exposure during her pregnancy, you could end up with some of these other effects happening.",
"NEW3-028": "False. Using idiosyncratic for every reaction that happens with low frequency is not the proper use of the term. Idiosyncratic toxicity is a genetically determined abnormal reactivity to a chemical. The person's response is qualitatively similar to other people's, but extreme. So rarity alone does not make a reaction idiosyncratic. In class she described it as an unexpected, harsh reaction that we could not explain. Pharmacogenomics may help explain idiosyncratic responses. A person with a rare genetic variant may metabolize a drug differently from everyone else and form a hepatotoxic metabolite, so the reaction becomes explainable. The liver is the organ most likely to have idiosyncratic reactions.",
"NEW3-029": "False. Hepatotoxins do not always injure zone three. Liver injury is site specific, for two reasons. First, oxygen: it is highest in zone one, next to the inflow vessels, and lowest in zone three, next to the central venule, so an enzyme that needs oxygen to make a toxic metabolite injures zone one. Second, enzyme location: many phase two enzymes are in zone one, and cytochrome P four fifty two E one is higher in zone three. That is why acetaminophen, made into N A P Q I by that enzyme, injures zone three. In class she said, a lot of our phase two enzymes are in zone one. And some of our P four fifties are more in zone three. Acetaminophen is one example, not the rule.",
"NEW3-030": "True. The blood-brain barrier is still developing at birth, for several months to years. The barrier limits what reaches the brain: compounds must pass into the endothelial cell membranes, transporters send some back to the blood, and lipophilic compounds get in more easily. In a newborn that barrier is not finished, so a medication that does not reach an adult's brain might reach a baby's brain. In class she said, that blood-brain barrier is still developing at birth for several months to years. Therefore, we have to be very careful when we expose kids to certain medications.",
"NEW3-031": "True. Remyelination is much less effective in the central nervous system than in the peripheral nervous system. Myelinopathy is disruption of myelin or injury to the cells that make it. In the peripheral nervous system those cells are Schwann cells; in the central nervous system they are oligodendrocytes. After injury, the myelinating cells can divide and cover the bare axon again, but this takes a long time, and in the central nervous system it works much less well. The trap is reversing the two systems. Repair is better in the periphery, which fits the general point that central nervous system injury tends to be permanent.",
"NEW3-032": "False. Activated charcoal does not bind every compound a person might ingest. Its chemistry lets it grab only certain kinds of compounds. Single-dose charcoal adsorbs those compounds while they are still in the gut, and what it binds leaves in the feces. It works best soon after the ingestion. Multiple-dose charcoal is limited further, to compounds that both stick to charcoal and undergo enterohepatic recirculation. In class she said, not every compound a person ingests is going to be grabbed and absorbed by charcoal. So, can't use it for everything.",
"NEW3-033": "False. Metabolizing enzymes are not induced to treat a poisoning. Induction takes a few days, and a poisoned patient cannot wait that long. To enhance metabolism, cofactors are given to speed up the enzymes' reactions instead. Her two examples are cyanide, where sodium thiosulfate is given for the body's own enzyme rhodanese, and acetaminophen, where N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione. In class she said, you'll see tables of which medication will induce which enzyme, but we can't do it fast enough. It takes a few days. The statement that metabolism can be enhanced by inducing enzymes is the classic wrong one.",
"NEW3-034": "True. Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate is very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital, which are both weak acids. Sodium bicarbonate raises the pH of the urine, so the weakly acidic toxin becomes charged there. The charged form is not reabsorbed by the tubules and leaves in the urine. This is one way to enhance renal elimination. It is not done for weakly basic toxins, because it would mean acidifying the blood. The trap is mixing up the example pairs: methanol and ethylene glycol belong to inhibiting bioactivation, and cyanide and acetaminophen to enhancing metabolism.",
"NEW3-035": "False. Inactivation is not limited to poison still in the gut. It is done to reduce the activity of toxins that have already entered the circulation and are not readily eliminated. The inactivator binds the toxin and keeps it from reaching its target tissues, and the complex is then cleared. The inactivator must have high affinity for the toxin, and the complex must have low toxicity; she said it should have no toxicity. Examples are the heavy metal chelators and the antivenoms and antibodies, such as digoxin immune Fab. Keeping a poison in the gut before it is absorbed is a different method, prevention of absorption, which is toxicokinetic.",
"NEW3-036": "True. Pralidoxime cannot pull an aged organophosphate off acetylcholinesterase. Pralidoxime removes the toxin from the active site: it pulls the organophosphate off the enzyme onto itself. Some organophosphates, because of their chemical structure, lose a part after they bind. Then the enzyme is permanently bound. In class she said, the acetylcholinesterase is permanently bound by that organophosphate, and we can't pull it off the acetylcholinesterase using pralidoxime. That's called aging of your organophosphate. Atropine, the other antidote, is a muscarinic receptor antagonist that antagonizes the excess acetylcholine.",
"NEW3-037": "False. N-acetylcysteine loses effectiveness about eight hours after an acetaminophen overdose, but it should still be given past that point. N-acetylcysteine works by replenishing glutathione. Glutathione, with the help of the glutathione S-transferase enzymes, conjugates N A P Q I, the liver-toxic metabolite. An overdose uses up the body's glutathione, and N-acetylcysteine supplies what is needed to make more. Acetaminophen overdose shows nausea and vomiting and clinical signs of liver failure, and acetaminophen levels can be measured in the blood. The trap is the word never. Lower effectiveness after eight hours is not a reason to withhold the antidote.",
"NEW3-038": "True. After digoxin-specific antibody fragments are given, the total serum digoxin concentration increases. The test counts free digoxin and digoxin bound to the fragments together. The fragments bind digoxin and the complex is eliminated by the kidneys, but while it circulates it is measured. So the level cannot be used to calculate more doses of the fragments. The antidote is ovine, made from sheep antibody fragments. Digoxin overdose shows bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, and nausea and vomiting. The trap is expecting the level to fall once the antidote binds the drug.",
"NEW3-039": "True. A genetic variation present in less than one percent of a population is a rare variant. A variation present in at least one percent is a polymorphism. The cutoff is frequency, not the kind of change. The term rare variant is newer. It came into use as patients with bad drug responses were found to carry variations never seen before. In class she said, a rare variant is a genetic variation that is occurring in less than one percent of the population. The trap is reversing the cutoff: at or above one percent is a polymorphism, and below one percent is a rare variant.",
"NEW3-040": "False. A polymorphism is not limited to a single nucleotide change. Any genetic variation that occurs in at least one percent of a population is a polymorphism. A single nucleotide polymorphism, called a S N P, is the most common type. Other types include a change in more than one nucleotide, insertion or deletion of an entire gene, and extra copies of a gene. In class she said, any kind of genetic craziness that you can think of, if it happens in one percent or more of the population, by definition it's polymorphic. So the word is defined by how often the variation occurs, not by how big the change is.",
"NEW3-041": "False. An asymptomatic patient who took a modified-release calcium channel blocker does not always go to the hospital. The time window decides. For a modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil, a patient found within eighteen hours of ingestion goes to a hospital. Found after eighteen hours, call the Poison Center; the patient can probably be managed at home, and the Poison Center gives the follow-up. For modified-release verapamil the window is twenty four hours. In class she said, they're asymptomatic, and they ingested the modified release product, and you found them within eighteen hours of ingestion. You need to get him in. The window exists because extended-release tablets can clump into a bezoar that breaks apart at an unpredictable time. A patient who is symptomatic when found goes to the hospital whatever the time.",
"NEW3-042": "True. For pharmaceuticals in water, aquatic wildlife exposure is more significant than human exposure. Aquatic animals live in the water. They are exposed for their entire life, repeatedly, and constantly if their home range stays contaminated, by drinking the water, by eating food from the water, and through the skin. People are exposed mostly by drinking water, and how much depends on how and where they live. In class she said, how do they get exposed? Constantly, yeah. Drinking water levels are about parts per trillion, far below a therapeutic dose, while in wildlife, populations shifting toward female have been seen where estrogen levels are higher.",
"NEW3-043": "The incorrect pairing is organophosphates with flumazenil. Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote, a competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor. Organophosphate insecticides are treated with atropine and pralidoxime. The other pairings are correct. Opioids are treated with naloxone. Iron is treated with deferoxamine, which chelates the circulating ferric iron. Sulfonylureas and meglitinides are treated with octreotide, which inhibits insulin release from the pancreas. Other pairs to keep straight are acetaminophen with N-acetylcysteine, toxic alcohols with ethanol or fomepizole, and digoxin with digoxin-specific antibody fragments.",
"NEW3-044": "The incorrect pairing is pralidoxime with muscarinic receptor antagonism. That is atropine's mechanism. In organophosphate poisoning, atropine is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist, and pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase. The two organophosphate antidotes are given together, which makes their mechanisms easy to swap. The other pairings are correct. N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione. Fomepizole, like ethanol, inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase. Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas. Also keep straight that glucagon increases cyclic A M P in heart cells and that deferoxamine chelates circulating ferric iron.",
"NEW3-045": "The incorrect pairing is enhance metabolism with ethanol for ethylene glycol. Ethanol for ethylene glycol inhibits bioactivation. Methanol and ethylene glycol are bioactivated by alcohol dehydrogenase, and ethanol or fomepizole blocks that enzyme. The enhance-metabolism examples are cyanide, where sodium thiosulfate is given for the body's enzyme rhodanese, and acetaminophen, where N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione. The other pairings are correct. Activated charcoal prevents absorption. Fomepizole for methanol inhibits bioactivation. Sodium bicarbonate for salicylates enhances renal elimination by ion trapping.",
"NEW3-046": "The incorrect pairing is flumazenil with removing the toxin from the active site. Flumazenil is a receptor antagonist. In class she said, flumazenil is the antagonist. It's antagonizing the GABA receptor, which the benzos are agonizing. Treatments that remove the toxin from the active site are pralidoxime for organophosphates, high-concentration oxygen for carbon monoxide, and methemoglobin pulling cyanide away in cyanide poisoning. The other pairings are correct. Naloxone for opioids is a receptor antagonist. Physostigmine for jimsonweed acts like a receptor agonist, because it blocks acetylcholinesterase and raises acetylcholine. Vitamin K for warfarin by-passes an inhibited pathway.",
"NEW3-047": "The incorrect pairing is deferasirox with lead. Deferasirox chelates iron; it is the orally bioavailable iron chelator. The other pairings are correct. Deferoxamine chelates iron. Penicillamine chelates copper and is used with trientine in Wilson's disease. Succimer chelates lead, cadmium, mercury and arsenic. Edetate disodium chelates lead, and dimercaprol chelates gold, arsenic, lead and mercury. The trap is the two similar names. Deferoxamine and deferasirox both bind iron, and neither is a lead chelator.",
"NEW3-048": "The incorrect pairing is allele frequency defined as the frequency of a genotype. That is genotype frequency. Allele frequency is the relative frequency of one allele in a population. A genotype is the pair of alleles a person carries, so the two frequencies count different things. The other pairings are correct. Wild type is the version thought to be most common. A polymorphism is present in at least one percent of a population. A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci that co-exist on the same chromosome, and her own definition adds that these variants group together and co-exist with a higher frequency than would occur by chance.",
"NEW3-049": "The incorrect pairing is axonopathy with injury to the myelinating cells. Injury to the myelinating cells is myelinopathy. In axonopathy the axon itself is the primary site of injury and degenerates. The four patterns are neuronopathy, death of the entire neuron; axonopathy, degeneration of the axon; myelinopathy, disruption of myelin or injury to the myelinating cells; and toxicity to neurotransmission, which blocks excitation or causes excessive stimulation without killing the cell, as organophosphates do. Of neuronopathy she said, what did that neurotoxin do to this nerve cell? Killed the whole thing, right? Each name points to the part injured.",
"NEW3-050": "The incorrect pairing is propranolol asthma with an on-target dose that is too high. Worsening asthma with propranolol is off-target. It comes from activating an unintended receptor subtype, the beta two receptors in airway smooth muscle. The other pairings are correct. Sleepiness with diphenhydramine is on-target: the correct receptor in the wrong tissue. Arrhythmia with terfenadine is off-target, from inhibiting a cardiac potassium channel. Teratogenesis with racemic thalidomide is off-target, because the S enantiomer is a potent teratogen. An on-target dose that is too high comes from dosing errors, changes in pharmacokinetics, or a change in receptor number.",
"NEW3-051": "The incorrect pairing is a modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil with eight hours. Its window is eighteen hours; eight hours belongs to a sustained-release beta blocker. The windows for an asymptomatic patient are these. Immediate-release calcium channel blocker or beta blocker, except sotalol: six hours. Sustained-release beta blocker: eight hours. Modified-release calcium channel blocker except verapamil: eighteen hours. Modified-release verapamil: twenty four hours. Found within the window, get the patient to a hospital; found after it, call the Poison Center. Extended-release products get longer windows because they can form bezoars that break apart at unpredictable times.",
"NEW3-052": "This is opioid poisoning. The pattern is somnolence or coma, a decreased heart rate, a decreased breathing rate, and small pupils. The small pupils are the clue. Benzodiazepine overdose causes central nervous system depression with relatively normal vital signs. Tricyclic antidepressant overdose gives a fast heart rate and dilated pupils. Calcium channel blocker overdose gives low blood pressure and a slow heart rate, but the patient is often alert. The antidote for opioids is naloxone, a competitive antagonist of opioid receptors, and in a chronic opioid user it can trigger withdrawal. Increased pupil size is the finding that does not belong in the opioid toxidrome.",
"NEW3-053": "This is benzodiazepine poisoning. The pattern is central nervous system depression with relatively normal vital signs. The patient is deeply drowsy, but heart rate, blood pressure, breathing and pupils are normal. Opioid overdose also causes drowsiness, but it lowers the heart and breathing rates and shrinks the pupils. Beta-blocker overdose lowers blood pressure and heart rate. Salicylate overdose causes hyperventilation, a high temperature, confusion and seizures. Benzodiazepine levels can be measured in the blood. The antidote is flumazenil, a competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor, and in a patient dependent on benzodiazepines it can cause withdrawal.",
"NEW3-054": "This is most likely beta-blocker overdose. Beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose both cause low blood pressure and a slow heart rate. What separates them is mental status. Beta-blocker overdose may cause altered mental status, so a confused patient points to a beta blocker, while the calcium channel blocker patient is often alert. Beta-blocker overdose can also cause respiratory depression. Digoxin overdose gives a slow heart rate and arrhythmias, but with high serum potassium and nausea and vomiting. Tricyclic antidepressant overdose gives a fast heart rate, not a slow one. Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers share the same antidote list.",
"NEW3-055": "This is most likely calcium channel blocker overdose. The pattern is low blood pressure and a slow heart rate in a patient who is often alert. Beta-blocker overdose shares the low blood pressure and slow heart rate, but it may cause altered mental status, so an alert, talking patient points to the calcium channel blocker. Opioid overdose gives somnolence or coma with slow breathing and small pupils. Iron gives low blood pressure with a fast heart rate. The antidotes are the same as for beta blockers: atropine, calcium, glucagon, H I E T, vasopressors, and intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion. Glucagon works by increasing cyclic A M P in heart cells.",
"NEW3-056": "The antidote is digoxin-specific antibody fragments. Nausea and vomiting, a slow and irregular rhythm, and high serum potassium in a patient on a heart medication is the digoxin picture: bradycardia, arrhythmias, elevated serum potassium, and nausea and vomiting, with digoxin levels measurable in the blood. The antibody fragments bind digoxin, and the complex is eliminated by the kidneys. After they are given, total digoxin, free plus bound, goes up, so levels cannot guide more doses. Glucagon and calcium are antidotes for beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose, and atropine with pralidoxime is the organophosphate pair.",
"NEW3-057": "Here sodium bicarbonate is counteracting the inhibition of cardiac sodium channels. A fast heart rate, dilated pupils, dry mouth, agitation, seizures and an elongated Q R S interval is tricyclic antidepressant overdose, and sodium bicarbonate is given for the elongated Q R S interval. Intravenous fluids and dopamine or norepinephrine are mostly supportive care, to keep up blood pressure. The trap is that sodium bicarbonate has a second role: in salicylate overdose it works by renal ion trapping to enhance elimination. Increasing cyclic A M P in heart cells is glucagon, and acting as a sink for a lipophilic drug is intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion, both in beta-blocker overdose.",
"NEW3-058": "This is salicylate poisoning. The pattern is hyperventilation with respiratory alkalosis first, then metabolic acidosis, with hyperthermia, confusion and seizures. Fast, deep breathing followed later by an acidosis is the clue. Methanol also causes a metabolic acidosis, an anion-gap one, but with damage to the eyes and without the respiratory alkalosis first. Benzodiazepines cause central nervous system depression with relatively normal vital signs. Sulfonylureas cause low blood glucose. Treatment for salicylates is supportive care and intravenous sodium bicarbonate, which works by renal ion trapping to enhance elimination.",
"NEW3-059": "The listed antidote is octreotide. Sulfonylurea and meglitinide overdose causes hypoglycemia, low blood glucose, and the sweating and confusion fit that. Octreotide inhibits insulin release from the pancreas. Glucagon is on the antidote list for beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker overdose, where it increases cyclic A M P in heart cells to raise the heart rate. It is tempting here, but the listed antidote for sulfonylureas is octreotide. Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote, a competitive antagonist of the benzodiazepine receptor. Deferoxamine is the iron antidote, which chelates circulating ferric iron.",
"NEW3-060": "This is iron poisoning, and the time course is the clue. Within one to six hours there are vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea. Within six to twenty four hours come lethargy, coma, seizures, bloody vomiting, bloody diarrhea and shock, with low blood pressure and a fast heart rate. Liver dysfunction and failure are possible in two to five days. Acetaminophen also gives nausea and vomiting, then signs of liver failure, but not bloody vomiting with shock. Digoxin gives a slow heart and high potassium, and opioids slow the heart and breathing. The antidote for iron is deferoxamine, which chelates the circulating ferric iron.",
"NEW3-061": "Atropine here is a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist. Drooling, tearing, urination, diarrhea and vomiting in an insecticide sprayer is acetylcholine excess from organophosphate poisoning, the effects called SLUDGE: salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastric cramping and emesis. Atropine blocks the muscarinic receptors that the excess acetylcholine is stimulating. Pralidoxime is the second antidote, and it is the one that removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase, unless the organophosphate has aged. Competitive antagonism of opioid receptors is naloxone, and of the benzodiazepine receptor is flumazenil. The two organophosphate antidotes are the pair most often swapped.",
"NEW3-062": "N-acetylcysteine works by replenishing glutathione. In an acetaminophen overdose, more of the drug goes through cytochrome P four fifty two E one to N A P Q I, which damages the liver. Glutathione conjugates N A P Q I with the help of the glutathione S-transferase enzymes. The body makes glutathione all the time, but an overdose uses it up, and N-acetylcysteine supplies what is needed to make more. That is enhancing metabolism with a cofactor. It loses effectiveness about eight hours after the overdose but should still be given. Inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase is ethanol and fomepizole, chelating ferric iron is deferoxamine, and renal ion trapping is sodium bicarbonate for salicylates.",
"NEW3-063": "This is methanol poisoning. Methanol's active metabolites cause an anion-gap metabolic acidosis and ocular toxicity, so blurred vision after homemade liquor fits. Formic acid is the metabolite that damages the retina. Ethylene glycol is the other toxic alcohol, but its metabolites crystallize in the small blood vessels, and kidney toxicity is common. Salicylates cause hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis before the metabolic acidosis, with a high temperature. Iron causes vomiting, abdominal pain and diarrhea, then shock. The antidote for both toxic alcohols is ethanol or fomepizole, which inhibit alcohol dehydrogenase. Fomepizole is the stronger inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects.",
"NEW3-064": "The answer is bioactivation. Metabolism of a compound to something more harmful than what was taken is bioactivation. Some people call it toxication or metabolic activation. The opposite outcome, metabolism to something less harmful and easier to eliminate, is detoxification. Acetaminophen is her classic example: cytochrome P four fifty two E one turns it into N A P Q I, which is toxic to the liver. In class she said, I use the word bioactivation for that. Some people use the word toxication. Conjugation is usually detoxifying, not bioactivating. Hormesis is a dose-response shape, and potentiation is a type of chemical interaction, so neither describes what metabolism does to one compound.",
"NEW3-065": "The answer is cytochrome P four fifty two E one. Most of a normal acetaminophen dose is conjugated by phase two enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and the sulfotransferases, which is detoxification. When they cannot keep up, the rest goes down a phase one pathway, cytochrome P four fifty two E one, which makes N A P Q I, the metabolite that injures the liver. In class she said, specifically P four fifty two E one. We'll do this work to create that nasty N A P Q I. N A P Q I can form even below four grams a day if alcohol has induced this enzyme or if genetic variation lowers the phase two enzymes. A sulfotransferase detoxifies, and glutathione S-transferase helps conjugate N A P Q I; neither makes it.",
"NEW3-066": "The answer is Paracelsus. The saying that the dose makes the poison is attributed to him, and he studied the toxic effects of compounds long ago. In class she said, dose makes the poison. Who said that? Supposed to be attributed to a guy named Paracelsus. Dose is the fourth principle of toxicology: whether something poisons depends on how much is received. The lethal dose table from Casarett and Doull shows how much that varies, from ethyl alcohol at ten thousand milligrams per kilogram down to botulinum toxin. Casarett and Doull are the tempting answer, but they wrote the toxicology textbook; they are not the source of the saying. Vogel and Motulsky belong to the history of pharmacogenetics: Motulsky said inheritance might explain individual differences in drug efficacy and adverse reactions, and Vogel introduced the term pharmacogenetics in nineteen fifty nine.",
"NEW3-067": "The answer is three. In animal studies, chronic exposure is repeated exposure for more than three months, up to years. The four durations are these. Acute is less than twenty four hours. Subacute is less than one month. Subchronic is one to three months. Chronic is more than three months. In class she said, subchronic is one to three months, and then chronic is more than three months up to years. The trap is the subchronic window. One to three months is subchronic, so chronic starts past three months. Six or twelve months of exposure would still be chronic, but neither is the cutoff.",
"NEW3-068": "The answer is target. The five principles of toxicology are source, exposure, A D M E T, dose, and target and mechanism of action. Target and mechanism asks where the substance acts in the body and how it causes harm. Two points go with it. The target of a toxic effect may not be the therapeutic target. And the organ with the highest concentration may not be the organ that is damaged; the dose at the target determines toxicity. In class she said, sometimes the target of something toxic is not the same target as its therapeutic target. Route is the trap, but route belongs to the exposure principle.",
"NEW3-069": "The answer is E D fifty. The therapeutic index is the T D fifty over the E D fifty: the dose toxic to fifty percent of the animals divided by the dose effective in fifty percent. In general it is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose. Another percentage can be used, but the numerator and denominator must use the same one. In class she said, it doesn't always have to be fifty percent. It could be a different percentage, but the numerator and the denominator need to have the same percentage. E D ninety nine is the trap: T D one over E D ninety nine is the margin of safety, which uses two different percentages.",
"NEW3-070": "The answer is E D ninety nine. The margin of safety is the T D one over the E D ninety nine: the dose toxic to one percent of the animals divided by the dose effective in ninety nine percent. Because the two percentages differ, the value gives an idea of the slope of the dose-response curves. The therapeutic index, T D fifty over E D fifty, uses one point on each curve and carries no slope information. In class she said, what's the toxic dose for one percent of the animals over the effective dose of ninety nine percent of the animals. That's the margin of safety. E D fifty is the trap, since it belongs to the therapeutic index.",
"NEW3-071": "The answer is the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level. The margin of exposure is the N O A E L divided by the estimated exposure. The N O A E L comes from animal studies: it is the highest dose tested at which the animals showed no adverse effect. The exposure is the amount people, or wildlife, are estimated to receive. The margin of exposure is used for repeated exposures to compounds that are not therapeutic, such as a pollutant, which have no therapeutic dose. In class she said, the margin of exposure is defined as the Noel over the chronic exposure. Noel is how she pronounces N O A E L.",
"NEW3-072": "The answer is dispositional. Tolerance is decreased responsiveness after a prior exposure, and it has two mechanisms. Dispositional tolerance is based on A D M E: less of the toxicant reaches the site where the effect is produced. Carbon tetrachloride inhibits its own metabolism, so less of its nastier metabolite forms. Cadmium induces a metal-carrying protein that helps the kidneys clear it. The other mechanism is reduced responsiveness of the tissue, for example down-regulation of receptors. In class she said, there's what we call dispositional tolerance, and then there's what we call reduced responsiveness type of tolerance.",
"NEW3-073": "The answer is selective. Selective toxicity means an exposure is lethal to one species but not to another. It is one of the variations in toxic effect. Species can differ qualitatively and quantitatively, which raises the question of how well results extrapolate from one species to another. Individuals also differ, because of differences in their genetic profile. In class she said, one of the terms is what we call selective toxicity. This means that an exposure could be lethal to one species, but not to another. Idiosyncratic is the tempting wrong term, but it describes an extreme, genetically determined reaction in one person, not a difference between species.",
"NEW3-074": "The answer is Kupffer cells. Kupffer cells are a kind of white blood cell that lives in the liver. They wait there to clean up damaged tissue and fight pathogens. While they do that, they release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species, which adds to the liver's susceptibility to hepatotoxins. They are found throughout the liver, not in one zone. In class she said, they're just a kind of white blood cell that resides in the liver, waiting to be called up. Schwann cells are a different cell; they make myelin in the peripheral nerves.",
"NEW3-075": "The answer is zone three. In the acinar model, zone one is closest to the vessels where blood comes into the liver, and zone three is closest to the central venule, where blood leaves. Zone two is in between. Oxygen is highest in zone one and lowest in zone three. Cytochrome P four fifty two E one is expressed more in zone three, which is why acetaminophen kills liver cells in zone three. In class she said, zone one is closest to the vessels where the blood comes into the liver, and zone three is closest to the vessel where the blood leaves the liver. The trap is reversing the zones: inflow is zone one, outflow is zone three.",
"NEW3-076": "The answer is neuronopathy. Neuronopathy is death of the entire neuron. Astrocytes often multiply afterward, which is called gliosis. The other patterns injure only part of the neuron or its function. In axonopathy the axon degenerates and the cell body survives. In myelinopathy the myelin or the cells that make it are injured. In toxicity to neurotransmission, signaling is disturbed without cell death. In class she said, what did that neurotoxin do to this nerve cell? Killed the whole thing, right? Axonopathy is the tempting answer, because the axon is lost in it too, but the cell body survives.",
"NEW3-077": "The answer is Schwann cells. In the peripheral nervous system, myelin is formed by Schwann cells. A Schwann cell encircles an axon and wraps around it again and again. In the central nervous system the myelinating cells are oligodendrocytes, which is the trap. Myelin is one reason the nervous system is susceptible to toxicants. Injury to myelin or to the myelinating cells is myelinopathy, and remyelination is much less effective in the central nervous system than in the peripheral nervous system. In class she said, these Schwann cells, they are formed around the axons of nerves, and they get very stretched and they wrap around and around and around the axon.",
"NEW3-078": "The answer is interstitial. Proton pump inhibitors, such as omeprazole, can cause interstitial nephritis. Interstitial nephritis is listed for antibiotics, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and diuretics, and she added the proton pump inhibitors. She described it as an allergic response to the drug that shows up as interstitial nephritis in some patients, not as hives or trouble breathing. The over-the-counter label says to limit use to two weeks. In class she said, your proton pump inhibitors can cause interstitial nephritis. The other kinds of renal damage are prerenal, vasoconstriction, crystalluria, tubular toxicity, endothelial injury and glomerulopathy.",
"NEW3-079": "The answer is glutathione. N A P Q I, the liver-toxic metabolite of acetaminophen, is conjugated by glutathione with the help of the glutathione S-transferase enzymes. The body makes glutathione all the time, but an overdose uses it up. N-acetylcysteine supplies what is needed to make more, which is why its mechanism is to replenish glutathione. In class she said, what compound grabs N A P Q I, conjugates it? Glutathione, with the help of the G S T enzymes. N-acetylcysteine is the tempting answer, but it is the antidote that rebuilds glutathione, not the compound that conjugates N A P Q I. Thiosulfate and rhodanese belong to the cyanide example.",
"NEW3-080": "The answer is N A P Q I. Cytochrome P four fifty two E one bioactivates acetaminophen to N A P Q I, which damages the liver, mainly in zone three, where that enzyme is highest. Glutathione conjugates N A P Q I, and N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione. In class she said, N A P Q I, that's that free radical, that's nasty, damages liver. The other metabolites that come up in poisoning belong to the toxic alcohols. Methanol is bioactivated to formaldehyde and then formic acid, which damages the retina. Ethylene glycol is bioactivated to glycolic acid and then oxalic acid. Methemoglobin belongs to the cyanide example.",
"NEW3-081": "The answer is alcohol dehydrogenase. Alcohol dehydrogenase bioactivates methanol to formaldehyde, which goes on to formic acid, and ethylene glycol to glycoaldehyde, which goes on to glycolic acid and oxalic acid. Ethanol and fomepizole block alcohol dehydrogenase, so this is the toxicokinetic method of inhibiting bioactivation. Fomepizole is the stronger inhibitor and has fewer adverse effects than ethanol. In class she said, both of these compounds, we can use compounds that will block the activity of the alcohol dehydrogenase enzyme. Aldehyde dehydrogenase is the trap. It is the next step for ethylene glycol, not the enzyme the antidotes block.",
"NEW3-082": "The answer is ion trapping. Intravenous sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic. A weakly acidic poison, such as a salicylate, becomes charged in that urine, and the charged form is not reabsorbed by the tubules, so it leaves the body. This enhances renal elimination, and it is very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital. It is not done for weakly basic poisons, because that would mean acidifying the blood. In class she said, it's safest to try to treat slightly acidic poisonings by ion trapping, rather than trying to treat slightly basic poisonings by ion trapping. Hemodialysis and hemoperfusion remove poison outside the body, and chelation is inactivation.",
"NEW3-083": "The answer is serum sickness. Antivenoms are antibodies against a venom. They are made by inoculating animals with small amounts of venom to get a humoral antibody response. They have high affinity and high specificity for what they bind. Their danger is a type three hypersensitivity reaction, which is called serum sickness. In class she said, a certain kind of hypersensitivity reaction called type three hypersensitivity, and that it is also called serum sickness. Anaphylaxis is the tempting answer, but it is not the reaction named for antivenoms. Aging belongs to organophosphates, and withdrawal to naloxone and flumazenil.",
"NEW3-084": "The answer is aging. Pralidoxime removes the toxin from the active site: it pulls the organophosphate off acetylcholinesterase. Some organophosphates, because of their chemical structure, lose a part after they bind. Once that happens, the enzyme is permanently bound, and pralidoxime can no longer pull the organophosphate off. In class she said, we can't pull it off the acetylcholinesterase using pralidoxime. That's called aging of your organophosphate. Atropine, the muscarinic receptor antagonist, still antagonizes the excess acetylcholine. Chelation is binding a metal, bioactivation is metabolism to a more harmful form, and conjugation is usually a detoxifying reaction.",
"NEW3-085": "The answer is eight. N-acetylcysteine loses effectiveness about eight hours after an acetaminophen overdose, and her key says it should still be given past that point. It works by replenishing glutathione: it supplies ingredients the body uses to make more glutathione, which conjugates N A P Q I, the metabolite that damages the liver. Acetaminophen overdose shows nausea and vomiting and clinical signs of liver failure, and acetaminophen levels can be measured in the blood. The other numbers belong elsewhere. Six, eighteen and twenty four hours are hospital windows for asymptomatic calcium channel blocker and beta-blocker patients, and eight hours is also the sustained-release beta-blocker window. Twelve to twenty four hours is the beta-blocker follow-up period, and two hours is the outer limit for single-dose charcoal. For acetaminophen, eight hours is when the antidote loses effectiveness, not when it stops being given.",
"NEW3-086": "The answer is sink. In beta-blocker overdose, intravenous twenty percent fatty acid emulsion acts as a sink for the drug if the drug is lipophilic enough. Each of the other beta-blocker antidotes has its own mechanism. Atropine increases heart rate. Calcium tries to drive calcium into heart cells. Glucagon increases cyclic A M P in heart cells to raise the heart rate. For H I E T, the mechanisms of its inotropic effects are not understood. Vasopressors increase blood pressure. The same list is used for calcium channel blocker overdose. Chelator and antibody describe inactivators, not the fat emulsion.",
"NEW3-087": "The answer is six. An asymptomatic patient who took an immediate-release calcium channel blocker and is found within six hours of ingestion goes to a hospital. Found after six hours, call the Poison Center; the patient can probably be managed at home. The same six-hour window applies to immediate-release beta blockers, except sotalol. Immediate-release products are absorbed more rapidly because they do not tend to form bezoars. Extended-release products can form bezoars that break apart at unpredictable times, so their windows are longer: eight hours for a sustained-release beta blocker, eighteen for a modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil, and twenty four for modified-release verapamil.",
"NEW3-088": "The answer is eight. An asymptomatic patient who took a sustained-release beta blocker and is found within eight hours of ingestion goes to a hospital. After eight hours, call the Poison Center. In class she said, same thing with the beta blockers, a sustained release product, but the time frame for this is within eight hours. You got to get him to the hospital. The window is longer than the six hours for immediate-release products because extended-release products tend to form bezoars that can break apart at unpredictable times, so no one can be sure when the drug will be absorbed. Eighteen hours is the trap; it belongs to modified-release calcium channel blockers other than verapamil.",
"NEW3-089": "The answer is eighteen. An asymptomatic patient who took a modified-release calcium channel blocker other than verapamil and is found within eighteen hours of ingestion goes to a hospital. After eighteen hours, call the Poison Center; the patient can probably be managed at home, and the Poison Center gives the follow-up. In class she said, they're asymptomatic, and they ingested the modified release product, and you found them within eighteen hours of ingestion. You need to get him in. Verapamil is the exception, with twenty four hours. Eight hours is the trap; that is the sustained-release beta blocker.",
"NEW3-090": "The answer is twenty four. An asymptomatic patient who took modified-release verapamil and is found within twenty four hours of ingestion goes to a hospital. After twenty four hours, call the Poison Center. Verapamil gets a longer window than the other modified-release calcium channel blockers, which have eighteen hours. In class she said, if you found a person that's ingested modified release verapamil, and you found them within twenty four hours of ingestion, you still get them to the hospital. She also said verapamil has multiple mechanisms, so it is not as clean a calcium channel blocker as the others. A symptomatic patient goes to the hospital at any time, by ambulance.",
"NEW3-091": "The answer is haplotype. A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci, or areas of a gene, that co-exist on the same chromosome. In class she said, it just means it's a set of alleles at multiple loci that coexist in the same chromosome. Her own definition adds that the variants group together and co-exist with a higher frequency than would occur by chance; the term for that higher-than-chance frequency is linkage disequilibrium. A genotype is different: it is the two alleles a person carries for one gene. An allele is one version of a gene, and a polymorphism is a variation present in at least one percent of a population.",
"NEW3-092": "The answer is one. A genetic variation present in at least one percent of a population is a polymorphism. Below one percent, it is a rare variant. The definition depends on frequency, not on the kind of change, so a single nucleotide, several nucleotides, or a whole gene inserted, deleted or copied all count if they reach one percent. In class she said, if you find a genetic variant and it's occurring in at least one percent of your population, it's a polymorphism. Five or ten percent are traps; the cutoff is one percent. A single nucleotide polymorphism is the most common type of polymorphism, not its definition.",
"NEW3-093": "The answer is linkage disequilibrium. A haplotype is a set of alleles at multiple loci that co-exist on the same chromosome. When that set of alleles co-exists more often than random chance would predict, the alleles are said to be in linkage disequilibrium. In class she put it this way: a haplotype has a statistical significance, and it occurs at a frequency greater than random chance. Her own definition of a haplotype includes that higher-than-chance frequency, but the name for the state itself is linkage disequilibrium. Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is the trap. It means allele and genotype frequencies stay constant from generation to generation unless specific influences are introduced. Heterozygosity and homozygosity describe the two alleles at one locus in one person, and genotype frequency is how common a genotype is in a population.",
"NEW3-094": "The answer is heterozygous. Humans are diploid: each person has two copies of each chromosome, with one allele from the biological mother and one from the biological father. If the two alleles at a locus are the same, the person is homozygous at that locus. If they are different, the person is heterozygous. In class she said, so they are what, homozygous or heterozygous. They're heterozygous, right? Homozygous is the trap, the term for two identical alleles. Diploid describes having two copies of each chromosome, not whether the alleles match.",
"GAP3-001": "Dextromethorphan, valproic acid and methylphenidate all have evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management. Digoxin and benzodiazepines do not. Fifteen drugs and compounds have these guidelines: acetaminophen, atypical antipsychotics, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, dextromethorphan, diphenhydramine and dimenhydrinate, iron, methylphenidate, salicylate, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants and valproic acid, then three non-drug compounds, elemental mercury, ethylene glycol and long-acting anticoagulant rodenticides. Digoxin and benzodiazepines are taught elsewhere, with digoxin immune Fab and flumazenil. In class she said, only a few drugs are out there that have these documents written, but these are pretty nasty drugs.",
"GAP3-002": "Elemental mercury, ethylene glycol and long-acting anticoagulant rodenticides are the three non-drug compounds with evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management. Methanol and organophosphate pesticides are not on the list. Methanol is the strong trap. It is taught beside ethylene glycol, because both are bioactivated by alcohol dehydrogenase and both are treated with ethanol or fomepizole, but only ethylene glycol has a guideline. Organophosphates are taught as the poisoning treated with atropine and pralidoxime, not as a guideline compound. The twelve drugs on the same list include acetaminophen, iron, salicylate, the beta blockers and the calcium channel blockers.",
"GAP3-003": "Cyanide is the exception; it is not on the list of evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management. Salicylate, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and atypical antipsychotics are all on it. The full list is acetaminophen, atypical antipsychotics, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers, dextromethorphan, diphenhydramine and dimenhydrinate, iron, methylphenidate, salicylate, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants, valproic acid, elemental mercury, ethylene glycol and long-acting anticoagulant rodenticides. Cyanide is taught elsewhere: as the example of enhancing metabolism, where sodium thiosulfate is given for the body's enzyme rhodanese, and as a toxin removed from its active site.",
"GAP3-004": "Diphenhydramine and dimenhydrinate share a single entry on the guideline list. Every other entry names one drug or class. Beta blockers and calcium channel blockers are the tempting answer, because they share one antidote list, but each has its own guideline document. Methanol and ethylene glycol are paired in the bioactivation examples, but only ethylene glycol is on the guideline list. Acetaminophen and salicylate are both on the list, as two separate entries. The other entries are atypical antipsychotics, dextromethorphan, iron, methylphenidate, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, tricyclic antidepressants, valproic acid, elemental mercury and long-acting anticoagulant rodenticides.",
"GAP3-005": "The cholinergic and opioid toxidromes include decreased pupil size. A toxidrome is a group of symptoms that points to a kind of exposure. The cholinergic toxidrome, from organophosphates, has somnolence or coma, an increased respiratory rate, small pupils, and the effects called SLUDGE with fasciculation. The opioid toxidrome, from heroin or oxycodone, has somnolence or coma with decreased heart rate, breathing rate and pupil size. Pupils are increased in the anticholinergic and sympathomimetic toxidromes, so anticholinergic goes the opposite way. The sedative-hypnotic toxidrome, from benzodiazepines and barbiturates, has no change in pupil size. Increased pupil size never belongs to the opioid toxidrome.",
"GAP3-006": "This is the anticholinergic toxidrome, from drugs such as diphenhydramine and atropine. Its pattern is delirium; increased heart rate, blood pressure, temperature and pupil size; and ileus and flushing. The sympathomimetic toxidrome, from cocaine or amphetamine, has the same four increases, which makes it the trap. The difference is the mental status and the other signs. Sympathomimetic patients are agitated, with tremor and sweating, called diaphoresis. Anticholinergic patients are delirious, with ileus and flushing. The salicylate toxidrome has confusion, sweating and vomiting, and the cholinergic toxidrome has small pupils and SLUDGE.",
"GAP3-007": "An increased heart rate is part of the sympathomimetic, anticholinergic and salicylate toxidromes. The sympathomimetic toxidrome, from cocaine or amphetamine, and the anticholinergic toxidrome, from diphenhydramine or atropine, both raise heart rate, blood pressure, temperature and pupil size. The salicylate toxidrome, from aspirin, raises heart rate, breathing rate and temperature. Heart rate is decreased in the opioid toxidrome and in the calcium channel blocker toxidrome, from verapamil. The cholinergic and sedative-hypnotic toxidromes have no heart rate change. Calcium channel blockers are the trap because they are a heart drug, but in overdose they slow the heart and lower blood pressure.",
"GAP3-008": "Salivation, urination and emesis are among the SLUDGE effects. SLUDGE stands for the muscarinic effects of salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, gastric cramping and emesis. They are the other signs of the cholinergic toxidrome, from organophosphates, where acetylcholine is in excess. Fasciculation is listed next to SLUDGE in the cholinergic toxidrome, but it is not one of the SLUDGE effects. Flushing belongs to the anticholinergic toxidrome, together with ileus, which is the opposite picture. The cholinergic toxidrome also has somnolence or coma, an increased respiratory rate and small pupils. Atropine, a muscarinic receptor antagonist, treats the muscarinic effects.",
"GAP3-009": "Blood pressure and respiratory rate are decreased in the sedative-hypnotic toxidrome, from benzodiazepines and barbiturates. The pattern is somnolence or coma with lower blood pressure and slower breathing, and no change in heart rate, temperature or pupil size. The opioid toxidrome is the trap. It shares somnolence or coma and the slower breathing, but it also lowers heart rate and shrinks the pupils, with no blood pressure change. A decreased heart rate belongs to the opioid and calcium channel blocker toxidromes, and small pupils to the opioid and cholinergic toxidromes. For benzodiazepines alone, the description is central nervous system depression with relatively normal vital signs.",
"GAP3-010": "The calcium channel blocker toxidrome, from verapamil, is decreased heart rate and decreased blood pressure. Those are its only two findings, both down, with no mental status entry. Other toxidromes also have decreases, and each pair belongs to one of them. Decreased heart rate and respiratory rate is the opioid toxidrome, which also has small pupils. Decreased blood pressure and respiratory rate is the sedative-hypnotic toxidrome. Increased heart rate and respiratory rate, with increased temperature, is the salicylate toxidrome. Calcium channel blocker overdose shows low blood pressure and a slow heart rate, often in an alert patient.",
"GAP3-011": "The opioid toxidrome is somnolence or coma with decreased heart rate, respiratory rate and pupil size. Three toxidromes share somnolence or coma, so the vital signs separate them. Opioids, such as heroin or oxycodone, lower heart rate, breathing rate and pupil size. The sedative-hypnotic toxidrome, from benzodiazepines and barbiturates, lowers blood pressure and breathing rate. The cholinergic toxidrome, from organophosphates, raises the breathing rate and shrinks the pupils, with SLUDGE and fasciculation. Confusion with increased heart rate, breathing rate and temperature is the salicylate toxidrome. Increased pupil size is never part of the opioid toxidrome.",
"GAP3-012": "A large aspirin overdose gives the salicylate toxidrome: confusion; increased heart rate, respiratory rate and temperature; and sweating and vomiting, with no change in blood pressure or pupil size. It is the only toxidrome with an increased breathing rate together with an increased temperature. Agitation with tremor and increased heart rate, blood pressure, temperature and pupils is the sympathomimetic toxidrome. Delirium with flushing and the same four increases is the anticholinergic toxidrome. Somnolence with fast breathing, small pupils and fasciculation is the cholinergic toxidrome. Salicylate poisoning is treated with supportive care and intravenous sodium bicarbonate.",
"GAP3-013": "Three statements are correct: ion trapping prevents tubular reabsorption, sodium bicarbonate is given for a weakly acidic toxin, and it is very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital. The toxin is filtered at the glomerulus and would be reabsorbed from the forming urine. Sodium bicarbonate makes the urine more basic, so a weakly acidic toxin becomes charged and stays in the urine. Filtration does not change; reabsorption is what is blocked. It is not done for weakly basic toxins. In class she said, it's safest to try to treat slightly acidic poisonings by ion trapping, rather than trying to treat slightly basic poisonings by ion trapping.",
"GAP3-014": "Ion trapping with sodium bicarbonate is very effective for salicylates and phenobarbital, which are both weak acids. Each treatment method has its own examples, and the other pairs belong to other methods. Methanol and ethylene glycol are the examples of inhibiting bioactivation, where ethanol or fomepizole blocks alcohol dehydrogenase. Cyanide and acetaminophen are the examples of enhancing metabolism by giving cofactors, sodium thiosulfate and N-acetylcysteine. Organophosphates and carbon monoxide are examples of removing a toxin from its active site, with pralidoxime and high-concentration oxygen. Ion trapping is one way to enhance renal elimination.",
"GAP3-015": "Digoxin immune Fab is the antibody antidote for a drug overdose. It is made of ovine antibody fragments, from sheep, that bind digoxin, and the complex is eliminated by the kidneys. It belongs to inactivation of poisons, with the antivenoms and antibodies. In class she said, we actually have one antibody that treats a drug overdose. Deferoxamine is also an inactivator, but it is a chelating agent that binds iron, not an antibody. Pralidoxime removes the organophosphate from acetylcholinesterase, a pharmacological treatment. Fomepizole inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase, which inhibits bioactivation of methanol and ethylene glycol.",
"GAP3-016": "Three statements are correct: antivenoms and antibodies have high affinity and high specificity, antivenoms are made by inoculating animals with small amounts of venom, and their danger is a type three hypersensitivity reaction, serum sickness. Antivenoms are antibodies against a venom, produced through a humoral antibody response in the inoculated animal. In class she said, an anti-venom or an antibody has to have high affinity and high specificity for whatever it is trying to grab out of your body. Low specificity is wrong; specificity is high. Binding metals through a nucleophilic electron donor describes the heavy metal chelators, the other kind of inactivator.",
"GAP3-017": "Most of the time, a neurotoxin produces one discrete kind of neurotoxicity. The kinds are neuronopathy, death of the whole neuron; axonopathy, degeneration of the axon; myelinopathy, injury to myelin or the cells that make it; and interrupted neurotransmission, as with organophosphates, without cell death. They are separate kinds of injury, not stages that follow one another. In class she said, most of the time, a neurotoxin that you might be exposed to is gonna cause one of these discrete kinds of neurotoxicity. It's very rare that you're exposed to something that will completely blast apart the myelin, mess up your neurotransmission, and also kill the axon.",
"GAP3-018": "False. A single neurotoxin usually does not destroy myelin, disturb neurotransmission and kill the axon at the same time. Most of the time, a neurotoxin causes one discrete kind of neurotoxicity: neuronopathy, axonopathy, myelinopathy, or interrupted neurotransmission. One compound that does all of those things together is very rare. In class she said, it's very rare that you're exposed to something that will completely blast apart the myelin, mess up your neurotransmission, and also kill the axon. The statement describes the rare case as if it were the usual one, which is what makes it false.",
"GAP3-019": "Antidepressants, verapamil and antipsychotics have a large volume of distribution. The drugs with a volume of distribution greater than five liters per kilogram are antidepressants, antipsychotics, antimalarials, opioids, propranolol and verapamil. An overdose of these is hard to remove from the blood, for example by hemodialysis, because most of the drug is sequestered in lipid, muscle or bone and must move back into the blood before dialysis can reach it. In class she said, overdose drugs with large volumes of distribution, they're hard to remove from the blood. Salicylates are the trap; they are the example for ion trapping, and ethylene glycol is the example for inhibiting bioactivation.",
"GAP3-020": "The property is a volume of distribution greater than five liters per kilogram. Antidepressants, antipsychotics, opioids and verapamil, along with antimalarials and propranolol, distribute out of the blood and are sequestered in lipid, muscle or bone. Hemodialysis only removes drug from the blood, so these overdoses are hard to remove that way. In class she said, it's not staying in the vasculature, right? It's distributed and probably sequestered somewhere like lipids, like muscle, like bone. The other overdose changes are real but concern clearance: exceeded protein binding raises the free drug, saturated first-pass metabolism lets more drug through, and zero-order elimination extends the half-life.",
"GAP3-021": "Toxicology is the study of adverse effects of chemicals in living organisms. In class she said, toxicology with that y at the end, it's the study of poisons. The other definitions describe kinds of substances, not the field. A toxin is a toxic substance produced by a biological system, such as snake venom. A toxicant is a toxic substance produced by, or a by-product of, human activities. A xenobiotic is a compound in an organism that the organism does not normally produce or expect to be present. A poison is any agent that can cause a deleterious response in a biological system. The trap is picking a substance definition in place of the definition of the field.",
"GAP3-022": "This is an additive interaction. In an additive interaction the combined response equals the sum of the responses to each chemical alone. Casarett and Doull write it as two plus three equals five, and two organophosphate insecticides inhibiting acetylcholinesterase are their example. The four interaction types are additive, synergistic, potentiation and antagonism. Synergism gives much more than the sum, two plus two equals twenty, as with carbon tetrachloride and ethanol. Potentiation is when one chemical has no effect alone but makes the other much more toxic, zero plus two equals ten, as with isopropanol and carbon tetrachloride. Antagonism gives less than expected, four plus six equals eight; four plus negative four equals zero and four plus zero equals one are antagonism too.",
"GAP3-023": "This is a synergistic interaction. Carbon tetrachloride and ethanol are both toxic to the liver, and together they cause much more liver injury than the sum of the two. Casarett and Doull write synergism as two plus two equals twenty. Potentiation is the trap. Potentiation also gives more than expected, but one of the two chemicals has no toxicity of its own on that tissue, zero plus two equals ten, as with isopropanol and carbon tetrachloride. Here both chemicals are hepatotoxic alone, so it is synergism. Additive would only equal the sum, and antagonism would give less than expected. The test is whether each chemical is toxic by itself.",
"GAP3-024": "This is potentiation. Isopropanol is not toxic to the liver on its own, yet it makes carbon tetrachloride much more hepatotoxic. Potentiation means a substance without toxicity on a tissue increases the toxicity of another chemical on that tissue. Casarett and Doull write it as zero plus two equals ten. Synergism is the trap. In synergism both chemicals are toxic alone and together give much more than the sum, two plus two equals twenty, as with carbon tetrachloride and ethanol. Additive would mean adding nothing to carbon tetrachloride's injury, leaving it unchanged. Antagonism would lower the toxicity, and here it went up.",
"GAP3-025": "This is antagonism. Two chemicals given together interfere with each other's actions, or one interferes with the other's, so the combined response is less than expected. Casarett and Doull give four plus six equals eight, four plus negative four equals zero, and four plus zero equals one. Additive is the plain sum, as in two plus three equals five, so four plus six would have to equal ten. Synergism, two plus two equals twenty, and potentiation, zero plus two equals ten, both give more than expected, not less. Antagonism is the basis of many antidotes. Naloxone antagonizes opioids at the opioid receptor, and atropine antagonizes acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors in organophosphate poisoning.",
"GAP3-026": "Three pairings are correct: additive is two plus three equals five, potentiation is zero plus two equals ten, and synergistic is two plus two equals twenty. Synergism and potentiation are the pair most often swapped. In synergism both chemicals have an effect alone, so the numbers start with two plus two. In potentiation one chemical has no effect alone, so the numbers start with zero. Two plus three equals five is additive, not antagonism; antagonism is less than expected, as in four plus six equals eight. In class she said, you could have additive or synergistic or potentiation or antagonistic type of interactions going on, which makes it really hard to predict how bad is it going to be.",
"GAP3-027": "The therapeutic index is twenty. The formula is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose, by convention the T D fifty divided by the E D fifty, with the same percentage on top and bottom. Here the T D fifty is two hundred milligrams per kilogram and the E D fifty is ten. Two hundred divided by ten is twenty. The traps use the wrong operation. Ten divided by two hundred, five hundredths, is the ratio upside down. Two hundred minus ten, one hundred ninety, is a difference, and two hundred plus ten, two hundred ten, is a sum; the index is a ratio. In class she said, most of the time we're using the toxic dose in fifty percent of the animals over the therapeutic dose of fifty percent of the animals.",
"GAP3-028": "The margin of safety is four. The formula is the T D one over the E D ninety nine: the dose toxic to one percent of the animals divided by the dose effective in ninety nine percent. Here the T D one is one hundred and the E D ninety nine is twenty five. One hundred divided by twenty five is four. The traps use the wrong doses. Two hundred over ten, which is twenty, is the therapeutic index. One hundred over ten, which is ten, mixes in the E D fifty where the E D ninety nine belongs. Twenty five over one hundred, zero point two five, is upside down. Because the margin of safety uses two different percentages, it reflects the slopes of the curves, which the therapeutic index does not.",
"GAP3-029": "The margin of exposure is one hundred. The formula is the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, divided by the estimated exposure. Here the N O A E L is fifty milligrams per kilogram per day, and the exposure is one half milligram per kilogram per day. Fifty divided by one half is one hundred. The traps use the wrong operation. One half divided by fifty, one hundredth, is upside down. Fifty times one half, twenty five, is a product, and fifty minus one half, forty nine and a half, is a difference. The N O A E L is the highest animal dose with no observed adverse effect. In class she said, the margin of exposure is defined as the Noel over the chronic exposure.",
"GAP3-030": "Drug B has the wider therapeutic index, twenty. The therapeutic index is the T D fifty divided by the E D fifty. Drug A: fifty divided by five is ten. Drug B: four hundred divided by twenty is twenty. Twenty is wider than ten, so Drug B has less chance of toxicity. Drug A is the trap: it works at a lower dose and so is more potent, but potency is not safety. Forty five is fifty minus five, a difference, and five hundredths is twenty over four hundred, upside down. In class she said, the real value of these numbers is that you can compare that value to other compounds' values and get an estimate for how bad is one compound compared to another.",
"GAP3-031": "Drug C has the wider margin of safety, three. The margin of safety is the T D one divided by the E D ninety nine. Drug C: sixty divided by twenty is three. Drug D: twenty divided by forty is one half, because its dose toxic to one percent is below its dose effective in ninety nine percent. Both drugs have the same therapeutic index, one hundred divided by ten, which is ten. That is the trap. Two would be forty divided by twenty, Drug D's ratio upside down. The therapeutic index uses one point on each curve and ignores the slope, so it cannot tell these drugs apart. The margin of safety uses two different percentages and so reflects the slopes. Two medications can have the same therapeutic index but unequal safe dosing.",
"GAP3-032": "Pollutant Y is the greater concern, with a margin of exposure of twenty. The margin of exposure is the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, divided by the exposure. Pollutant X: ten divided by one tenth is one hundred. Pollutant Y: two hundred divided by ten is twenty. The smaller margin means the exposure is closer to the dose that caused no adverse effect in animals, so Y is the bigger worry. X is the trap: its lower N O A E L makes it more toxic per dose, but its exposure is far below that dose. Zero point zero five is ten over two hundred, and zero point zero one is one tenth over ten; both are ratios turned upside down. In class she said, you'd calculate that margin of exposure and figure out which compound is more dangerous to humans being exposed to it.",
"L13-113": "Toxicologists pay particular attention to metabolism because it can turn a compound into something more harmful. Everything that gets into the body is absorbed or injected, distributed, likely metabolized, and excreted. Metabolism can go two ways. Detoxification makes something less harmful and easier to eliminate. Bioactivation, also called toxication or metabolic activation, makes something more noxious than the parent compound. Acetaminophen is her example: cytochrome P four fifty two E one makes N A P Q I, which injures the liver. In class she said, the metabolism part where a compound might be metabolized to something even nastier. I use the word bioactivation for that. Getting in is absorption, and removing the compound is excretion.",
"L13-114": "The conversion to N A P Q I is bioactivation because N A P Q I is more harmful than acetaminophen. Bioactivation is biotransformation of a potential poison to an active, harmful form; detoxification is biotransformation that removes the toxicant or prevents it from forming. Most of a normal dose is conjugated by the phase two enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and sulfotransferases, into a glucuronide or a sulfate, which is detoxification. The part that goes through cytochrome P four fifty two E one, a phase one enzyme, becomes N A P Q I, which is toxic to the liver. In class she said, if you go from acetaminophen through the P four fifty and create that N A P Q I, that is the bioactivation. N A P Q I comes from phase one, not phase two.",
"L14-101": "The therapeutic index is not used for a pollutant because a pollutant has no therapeutic dose to divide by. The therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose. A pollutant, or an occupational exposure, is not therapeutic. It can be toxic, so a toxic dose exists; what is missing is the therapeutic dose. For compounds that are not therapeutic, with repeated exposures, toxicology uses the margin of exposure: the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level from animal studies, over the exposure people are getting. In class she said, our margin of exposure is for non-therapeutic compounds with repeated exposures. The therapeutic index is for single doses.",
"L14-103": "A side effect unrelated to the drug's therapeutic mechanism most likely comes from the drug reaching an off-target receptor somewhere in the body. An off-target adverse effect is an undesired response from a drug acting at a receptor that is not its therapeutic target. Some side effects are the therapy gone too far, such as a diuretic removing too much fluid, and those look like the drug's own action. An unrelated effect cannot be explained that way. In class she said, it has this therapeutic mechanism of action, but it's causing this weird, completely unrelated side effect, it's cause that drug is going to this off target receptor somewhere in the body. Too much dose at the intended receptor is on-target.",
"L14-117": "Most toxins cause systemic effects because most are not highly reactive. A local effect happens at the site of first contact. Highly reactive agents, such as chlorine gas and acids, react where they touch and often never get the chance to go systemic. Most things people are exposed to are not that reactive. They get on the body, get into it, are absorbed at the site of entry, and are distributed, which produces systemic effects. In class she said, most toxins are not that reactive. Most things we're exposed to are not super reactive, so they get on us, they get in us, and then have to be absorbed. The trap reverses the requirement: systemic effects need absorption, and local ones do not.",
"L14-120": "The newer labeling includes male fertility because a drug's effect on sperm could contribute to teratogenicity. The Food and Drug Administration used to rank drugs on a scale from known to cause birth defects to not. The Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling Rule of two thousand fourteen added effects on female and male reproductive potential and the risks during breastfeeding, beside the risks during pregnancy. In class she said, if a guy is taking a certain medication, it may affect your sperm count, it may affect your sperm viability, and that could contribute to teratogenicity. Sperm do not carry the drug into the fetus; the concern is the effect on the sperm themselves.",
"L15-101": "Large inhaled particles impact at the branch points of the upper airway. They come in fast and cannot follow the turns where the bronchioles divide, so they hit those bifurcations and stay there. Particle size decides where a particle deposits. Medium-sized particles reach the alveolar regions of the lower lung, settling out as the breath stills, and very small particles are breathed back out. In class she said, the larger particles actually will get breathed in, and they don't get down into the lungs, because they are going to impact on the upper branches of your airway, so like where your bronchioles bifurcate. Solubility in water decides where a gas goes, not where a particle lands.",
"L15-110": "A large oral dose exposes the liver first because the portal circulation carries it straight there. Whatever is swallowed is absorbed from the gut into the portal blood, which reaches the liver before the rest of the body. The liver's job is to filter and clear the blood, so it takes up and concentrates compounds, and its metabolism can then make hepatotoxic metabolites on site. In class she said, if you ingest things orally, you've got all that portal circulation heading right to the liver. Size is the trap; she said, it's a large organ, yeah, that really doesn't have anything to do with it. Kupffer cells do not take drug from the gut, and bile flows out of the liver, not in.",
"L15-112": "Zone one can be the injured zone because enzymes there that need oxygen can make more toxic metabolite. Oxygen is highest in zone one, next to the inflow vessels, and lowest in zone three, next to the central venule. If a metabolizing enzyme needs oxygen to work, zone one supports more of that metabolism, and if the product is hepatotoxic, zone one is damaged. In class she said, if the enzymes that do metabolism there need oxygen to do their work, having a lot of oxygen right there is helpful to those enzymes, and unfortunately that metabolism may result in the metabolite that's hepatotoxic. Cytochrome P four fifty two E one is higher in zone three, which is why acetaminophen injures zone three instead.",
"L15-117": "Systemic effects require absorption because a systemic effect is carried through the bloodstream. A local effect happens where the substance first touches the body. A systemic effect happens somewhere else, so the substance must get into the blood at the site of entry and be distributed to where it acts. In class she said, to become systemic, that means it's going through the bloodstream, right? It's got to be absorbed. Systemic effects do require absorption from the site of contact. The trap mixes up duration with route. A single exposure can produce a systemic effect once the substance is absorbed and distributed; it does not need repeated exposure.",
"L15-118": "The effect returns each day because the worker has not developed tolerance. Tolerance is decreased responsiveness after a prior exposure. Without it, each day's exposure meets the same response, so the immediate effect, the headache or drowsiness, comes back every time. An exposure repeated day after day is chronic, but each day's dose can still cause an immediate effect. In class she said, even though that's been happening day after day, you may not develop any tolerance to it, and you may have an immediate effect, maybe drowsiness or a headache every time you walk into work. A delayed effect would appear weeks to years later, and an inhaled chemical's headache is not a local effect.",
"L15-119": "Drug A is more dangerous in overdose because the two drugs differ in therapeutic index. They share a mechanism and sedate at the same dose, but they differ in how the response continues as the dose rises. Drug A keeps rising in a straight line to coma, while drug B levels off near anesthesia, so B can be dosed higher without the unwanted effect. In class she said, even though they've got the same mechanism, they've got a different therapeutic index, which results in one drug being more dangerous than the other, based on the pharmacodynamics of it in overdose. This is a pharmacodynamic difference, so absorption and elimination order do not explain it.",
"L15-122": "Single-dose activated charcoal has to be given soon because, later, the drug has left the stomach and entered the blood. Charcoal can only bind drug that is still in the gut. Given early, it adsorbs the drug, and what it binds leaves in the feces. Given late, the drug has moved past the stomach and is being absorbed into the bloodstream, out of reach. In class she said, if you wait too long, that drug that somebody overdosed on is going to go past the stomach and start getting absorbed into the bloodstream. Her key gives less than sixty minutes after ingestion as the best time frame. In class she said up to two hours; they used to say just an hour. Charcoal itself is not absorbed."
});
